# Theme switching in `internal-transit`

## 1. The theme toggle component

**File: `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/Footer.astro`**

It is **`.astro`, not `.tsx`** — a plain `<button>` plus an Astro `<script>`. It lives in the footer, not a header/nav. Commit `d9255cd "Fixing cursor on theme switch button"` touched exactly one line here (added `cursor-pointer` to line 52).

Markup (lines 49–57):
```astro
<button
  id="theme-toggle"
  type="button"
  class="font-plex-sans text-portfolio-muted flex cursor-pointer items-center gap-1.5 text-sm"
>
  <span class="hidden dark:inline">Prefer the light?</span>
  <span class="inline dark:hidden">Prefer the dark?</span>
  <span class="font-semibold underline">Switch it up</span>
</button>
```

Handler (lines 60–88), verbatim:
```astro
<script>
  import { prefersReducedMotion } from "@/lib/animations";

  const button = document.getElementById("theme-toggle");

  function toggleTheme() {
    const isDark = document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark");
    localStorage.setItem("theme", isDark ? "dark" : "light");
  }

  // The View Transitions API snapshots the page before and after the
  // callback runs, so the .dark toggle inside it happens instantly and the
  // browser handles showing the correct theme on each side of the sweep —
  // no manual curtain/probe elements needed. The data attribute tells the
  // global CSS (in global.css) which of the two sweep-direction keyframes
  // to play against the new-theme screenshot.
  button?.addEventListener("click", () => {
    const goingDark = !document.documentElement.classList.contains("dark");
    document.documentElement.dataset.themeSweepDirection = goingDark
      ? "dark"
      : "light";

    if (!document.startViewTransition || prefersReducedMotion()) {
      toggleTheme();
      return;
    }
    document.startViewTransition(() => toggleTheme());
  });
</script>
```

So it does all three things you asked about:
- **Class on `<html>`**: `document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark")`
- **Data attribute on `<html>`**: `data-theme-sweep-direction="dark" | "light"` (set *before* the transition; it's what selects the keyframe). Note it is never cleaned up — it persists on `<html>` after the transition ends.
- **localStorage**: key `"theme"`, value `"dark"` | `"light"`

## 2. The current transition — View Transitions API + `clip-path: inset()` sweep

This is the "plain, simple line" the user is describing. It's a hard-edged horizontal line sweeping vertically over 500ms. Two halves:

**JS half** — `document.startViewTransition(() => toggleTheme())` in `Footer.astro` (above).

**CSS half** — `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/styles/global.css`, lines 296–341, verbatim:
```css
/* Theme-toggle sweep, driven by the View Transitions API (see the click
   handler in Footer.astro). The browser snapshots the page before and after
   the .dark class toggles into ::view-transition-old(root) and
   ::view-transition-new(root); overriding the default cross-fade with a
   clip-path keyframe reveals the new-theme screenshot as an expanding line
   sweeping across, with the old screenshot sitting untouched underneath —
   so each side of the line is already showing its correct final theme. */
::view-transition-old(root),
::view-transition-new(root) {
  animation: none;
  mix-blend-mode: normal;
}

::view-transition-old(root) {
  z-index: 1;
}

::view-transition-new(root) {
  z-index: 2;
}

[data-theme-sweep-direction="light"]::view-transition-new(root) {
  animation: theme-sweep-up 500ms ease-in-out forwards;
}

[data-theme-sweep-direction="dark"]::view-transition-new(root) {
  animation: theme-sweep-down 500ms ease-in-out forwards;
}

@keyframes theme-sweep-up {
  from {
    clip-path: inset(100% 0 0 0);
  }
  to {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
  }
}

@keyframes theme-sweep-down {
  from {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 100% 0);
  }
  to {
    clip-path: inset(0 0 0 0);
  }
}
```

Mechanics worth knowing for your replacement:
- The **new-theme snapshot** is the animated layer (`z-index: 2`), revealed over the static old snapshot (`z-index: 1`). Default cross-fade is killed with `animation: none; mix-blend-mode: normal`.
- Going **dark** sweeps top→bottom (`theme-sweep-down`, `inset(0 0 100% 0)` → full). Going **light** sweeps bottom→top (`theme-sweep-up`).
- There is **no overlay element, no mask, no motion/Framer involvement** in the real page transition today. Purely `::view-transition-*` pseudo-elements.
- The `[data-theme-sweep-direction=...]` selector attaches to `<html>`, and `::view-transition-*` pseudos are children of the document root, so the compound selector works.
- **Stale comment**: `global.css` line 126–128 in `:root` says `--portfolio-bg-light`/`--portfolio-bg-dark` are referenced by "the theme-sweep probes in Footer.astro" — those probes no longer exist (removed when the View Transitions rewrite happened). The two constants are still used by `--portfolio-bg` itself, though, and they're exactly the literal colors you need.

**Relevant for a pixelated dissolve:** `clip-path` on a `::view-transition-new(root)` pseudo can't easily do per-pixel noise. Practical routes: (a) swap `clip-path` for `mask-image` with an animated `mask-size`/dithered gradient on the same pseudo-element (masks are animatable on view-transition pseudos in Chromium), or (b) abandon the pseudo-element approach for a `position: fixed` canvas overlay that paints the dissolve (see §5).

## 3. Inline theme script (FOUC prevention)

**File: `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro`**, lines 29–39 — first thing in `<head>`, before the viewport meta and before any stylesheet:
```astro
<!-- Applies the dark theme before first paint so there's no flash of the
     wrong theme. Runs unhydrated/unbundled via is:inline, in document
     order, ahead of any stylesheet. Defaults to dark unless the visitor
     has explicitly chosen light before. -->
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var stored = localStorage.getItem("theme");
    var isDark = stored !== "light";
    document.documentElement.classList.toggle("dark", isDark);
  })();
</script>
```
**Site is dark-by-default** — anything other than the literal string `"light"` (including no stored value) resolves to dark. `prefers-color-scheme` is deliberately not consulted.

## 4. Global CSS / design tokens

**File: `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/styles/global.css`** (imported once, by `BaseLayout.astro` line 4). Tailwind v4, `@import "tailwindcss"`.

### Dark-mode scoping
Line 60 — a Tailwind v4 custom variant, keyed on a **`.dark` class** on `<html>`:
```css
@custom-variant dark (&:is(.dark *));
```
Raw values live in `:root` (light) and `.dark` (dark). No `[data-theme]`, no `@media (prefers-color-scheme)` anywhere in the codebase.

### Background colors (the literals you asked for)
Declared unconditionally in `:root` (lines 129–130) so both themes' values are always readable from either theme — ideal for a dissolve that needs both colors simultaneously:
```css
--portfolio-bg-light: #fff9f2;   /* warm off-white */
--portfolio-bg-dark:  #18181b;   /* zinc-900-ish near-black */
```
Then `--portfolio-bg: var(--portfolio-bg-light)` in `:root` (line 133), `--portfolio-bg: var(--portfolio-bg-dark)` in `.dark` (line 194).

Caveat: **dark mode also paints a gradient**, not just a flat color.
- `:root`: `--portfolio-bg-gradient: none;`
- `.dark`: `--portfolio-bg-gradient: linear-gradient(to bottom, #242429, #18181b);`

The `<body>` uses class `bg-portfolio-page` (BaseLayout line 64), defined at global.css 233–236:
```css
.bg-portfolio-page {
  background-color: var(--portfolio-bg);
  background-image: var(--portfolio-bg-gradient);
}
```
So the true dark background at the top of the viewport is `#242429`, fading to `#18181b` at the bottom. If your dissolve fills pixels with a flat `#18181b` it will read slightly too dark near the top of the page.

### Ink / muted
| Token | Light (`:root`) | Dark (`.dark`) |
|---|---|---|
| `--portfolio-ink` | `#18181b` | `#fff9f2` |
| `--portfolio-muted` | `#6b6b6b` | `#bbbbbb` |

### Accent / brand colors (for your "accent pixels")
Primary accent:
| Token | Light | Dark |
|---|---|---|
| `--portfolio-accent` | `#0f7a6b` (deep teal) | `#40c6b0` (bright teal) |

Full blog-category palette (light / dark pairs, lines 148–163 and 208–223) — a ready-made 8-hue sprinkle set:
| Category | Light | Light hover | Dark | Dark hover |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| design | `#4338ca` | `#3730a3` | `#818cf8` | `#a5b4fc` |
| philosophy | `#7e22ce` | `#6b21a8` | `#c084fc` | `#d8b4fe` |
| craft | `#c2410c` | `#9a3412` | `#fb923c` | `#fdba74` |
| creativity | `#854d0e` | `#713f12` | `#eab308` | `#facc15` |
| culture | `#0f766e` | `#115e59` | `#2dd4bf` | `#5eead4` |
| games | `#15803d` | `#166534` | `#4ade80` | `#86efac` |
| art | `#a21caf` | `#86198f` | `#e879f9` | `#f0abfc` |
| outdoors | `#b45309` | `#92400e` | `#fbbf24` | `#fcd34d` |

Also hardcoded social-brand hexes in `Footer.astro` (GitHub `#4183C4`, Figma `#FF3737`, LinkedIn `#0077B5`) — not tokens, probably not what you want.

Note the `@theme inline` block (lines 62–122) only maps `--color-portfolio-*` → `var(--portfolio-*)`; it holds no literals. To read hexes from JS at runtime, `getComputedStyle(document.documentElement).getPropertyValue("--portfolio-bg-light")` works from either theme.

Other files with their own token scopes (sandbox-local, not site theme): `src/styles/undercurrent-demo.css`, `src/styles/watchtower-demo.css`, `src/styles/watchtower-demo-editor.css`.

## 5. Layouts — where to mount a full-viewport overlay

`/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/`:
- **`BaseLayout.astro`** — the HTML shell. **Wraps every page.** `BlogLayout` and `ProjectLayout` both wrap `BaseLayout`, and `src/pages/index.astro` + `src/pages/blog/index.astro` use it directly. Nothing bypasses it.
- `BlogLayout.astro`, `ProjectLayout.astro` — content wrappers nested inside BaseLayout.
- `Footer.astro` — rendered by BaseLayout on every page.

**Best mount point:** inside `<body>` in `BaseLayout.astro`, as a sibling *after* `<Footer />` (around line 68), before the `<script>`/analytics. Body structure today:
```astro
<body class="bg-portfolio-page flex min-h-screen flex-col pt-16 font-mono">
  <div class="flex-1"><slot /></div>
  <Footer />
  ...
</body>
```
Since `<body>` is `flex flex-col`, a `fixed inset-0` overlay is out of flow and won't disturb that layout. **Z-index inventory across the whole codebase:** only `z-10` (×5), `z-20` (×1), and `z-50` (×1 — the Lightbox backdrop, `src/components/Lightbox.astro` line 16). So `z-[60]` or higher clears everything. You'll also want `pointer-events-none`.

If you keep the View Transitions approach instead, no DOM element is needed at all — but note view-transition pseudo-elements render *above* everything including `z-50`, so a fixed overlay is the only way to match that stacking outside the API.

## 6. React island patterns

`@astrojs/react` is configured in `astro.config.mjs` (plus `mdx`, `vercel` adapter, `sentry`). `motion@^12.42.2` and `recharts@2.15.4` are deps.

**Every single React island in the project uses `client:visible`** — and every one is mounted from an **MDX case-study file**, never from an `.astro` file:
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:84` — `<UndercurrentSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:97` — `<ThemeRevealSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:112` — `<DocsViewerSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/watchtower.mdx:99` — `<WatchtowerModulesSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/watchtower.mdx:123` — `<TerminalConversation client:visible />`

No `client:load`, `client:idle`, or `client:only` anywhere. `Lightbox.astro`'s header comment even states the house convention explicitly: interactive chrome is vanilla `<button>` + `<script>` with `getElementById`/`classList`, and React hydration is reserved for the case-study sandboxes. **Your theme wipe is site chrome, so the idiomatic choice here is vanilla JS in `BaseLayout.astro`/`Footer.astro`, not a React island** — a `client:load` island would also be the first one on the home page and would break that pattern.

**Canvas / requestAnimationFrame:** there is **exactly one** file using either — `src/components/Lightbox.astro` (it uses `requestAnimationFrame`, no `<canvas>`). **There is no `<canvas>` anywhere in the codebase.** So a canvas-based dissolve would be net-new territory; no house style to match.

**The closest stylistic precedent is `src/components/undercurrent-demo/ThemeRevealSandbox.tsx`** — a light/dark reveal with a draggable divider. It's genuinely relevant prior art: it stacks a full light copy under a full dark copy and animates `clip-path: inset(0 X% 0 0)` on the top layer via a `useMotionValue` + `useTransform` (deliberately bypassing React re-renders — motion writes straight to the DOM per frame). It uses `onPan` rather than `drag="x"` for reasons documented inline. It's a *demo* of the concept inside a case study, not the site's actual theme switcher, but it's the same conceptual model (two stacked theme copies, one clipped).

Other heavy-animation references, all motion/react and all in `src/components/watchtower-demo/`: `TerminalConversation.tsx`, `useTypewriter.ts`, `useStreamingReveal.ts`, `Bars.tsx`, `MeasureBar.tsx`.

## 7. prefers-reduced-motion

Yes, respected consistently, via three distinct patterns.

**Pattern A — shared JS helper** (`/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/lib/animations.ts`, lines 3–5):
```ts
export function prefersReducedMotion() {
  return window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
}
```
Used by `runFadeUpStagger` (same file, lines 11–16 — sets `opacity: 1` and returns early) and by the theme toggle in `Footer.astro:82`, where it **skips `startViewTransition` entirely** and just flips the class instantly. This is the pattern to preserve in your dissolve. `Lightbox.astro:110-111` re-declares the same function locally rather than importing it.

**Pattern B — CSS `no-preference` gate** (only animate when the user hasn't asked otherwise). `global.css:290-294`:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  [data-animate] {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}
```
Same shape in `src/pages/index.astro:177-184` (gates the project-row hover transitions).

**Pattern C — CSS `reduce` collapse-duration.** `src/components/Lightbox.astro:88-93` and `src/components/case-study/LightboxImage.astro:105`:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .lightbox-backdrop,
  .lightbox-content {
    transition-duration: 0.01ms;
  }
}
```

**Pattern D — React hook.** `useReducedMotion()` from `motion/react` in `TerminalConversation.tsx:242`, `useStreamingReveal.ts:41`, `useTypewriter.ts:52`, `DocsViewerSandbox.tsx:54`, `UndercurrentSandbox.tsx:23`.

Notably, the existing theme-sweep CSS in `global.css` (lines 303–341) has **no** reduced-motion guard of its own — it relies entirely on the JS early-return in `Footer.astro`. If you move to a CSS-driven or overlay-driven dissolve, keep that JS guard or add a CSS one.

---

## Summary of files you'll be touching

| File | Role |
|---|---|
| `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/Footer.astro` | Toggle button + click handler (`startViewTransition`, `data-theme-sweep-direction`, localStorage) |
| `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/styles/global.css` | `@theme inline` tokens, `:root`/`.dark` literals, `@custom-variant dark`, and the entire sweep animation (lines 296–341) |
| `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro` | Inline FOUC script (head), `<body>` — the mount point for a fixed overlay/canvas |
| `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/lib/animations.ts` | `prefersReducedMotion()` helper |
| `/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/components/undercurrent-demo/ThemeRevealSandbox.tsx` | Prior art: stacked light/dark copies + animated `clip-path` (demo only, do not break) |

Two gotchas to plan around: (1) `data-theme-sweep-direction` is set but never removed from `<html>`; (2) the dark background is a **gradient** (`#242429` → `#18181b`), so a flat-fill dissolve won't perfectly match the top of the page.

