## 5. Layouts — where to mount a full-viewport overlay

`/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/layouts/`:
- **`BaseLayout.astro`** — the HTML shell. **Wraps every page.** `BlogLayout` and `ProjectLayout` both wrap `BaseLayout`, and `src/pages/index.astro` + `src/pages/blog/index.astro` use it directly. Nothing bypasses it.
- `BlogLayout.astro`, `ProjectLayout.astro` — content wrappers nested inside BaseLayout.
- `Footer.astro` — rendered by BaseLayout on every page.

**Best mount point:** inside `<body>` in `BaseLayout.astro`, as a sibling *after* `<Footer />` (around line 68), before the `<script>`/analytics. Body structure today:
```astro
<body class="bg-portfolio-page flex min-h-screen flex-col pt-16 font-mono">
  <div class="flex-1"><slot /></div>
  <Footer />
  ...
</body>
```
Since `<body>` is `flex flex-col`, a `fixed inset-0` overlay is out of flow and won't disturb that layout. **Z-index inventory across the whole codebase:** only `z-10` (×5), `z-20` (×1), and `z-50` (×1 — the Lightbox backdrop, `src/components/Lightbox.astro` line 16). So `z-[60]` or higher clears everything. You'll also want `pointer-events-none`.

If you keep the View Transitions approach instead, no DOM element is needed at all — but note view-transition pseudo-elements render *above* everything including `z-50`, so a fixed overlay is the only way to match that stacking outside the API.

## 6. React island patterns

`@astrojs/react` is configured in `astro.config.mjs` (plus `mdx`, `vercel` adapter, `sentry`). `motion@^12.42.2` and `recharts@2.15.4` are deps.

**Every single React island in the project uses `client:visible`** — and every one is mounted from an **MDX case-study file**, never from an `.astro` file:
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:84` — `<UndercurrentSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:97` — `<ThemeRevealSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/undercurrent.mdx:112` — `<DocsViewerSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/watchtower.mdx:99` — `<WatchtowerModulesSandbox client:visible />`
- `src/content/projects/watchtower.mdx:123` — `<TerminalConversation client:visible />`

No `client:load`, `client:idle`, or `client:only` anywhere. `Lightbox.astro`'s header comment even states the house convention explicitly: interactive chrome is vanilla `<button>` + `<script>` with `getElementById`/`classList`, and React hydration is reserved for the case-study sandboxes. **Your theme wipe is site chrome, so the idiomatic choice here is vanilla JS in `BaseLayout.astro`/`Footer.astro`, not a React island** — a `client:load` island would also be the first one on the home page and would break that pattern.

**Canvas / requestAnimationFrame:** there is **exactly one** file using either — `src/components/Lightbox.astro` (it uses `requestAnimationFrame`, no `<canvas>`). **There is no `<canvas>` anywhere in the codebase.** So a canvas-based dissolve would be net-new territory; no house style to match.

**The closest stylistic precedent is `src/components/undercurrent-demo/ThemeRevealSandbox.tsx`** — a light/dark reveal with a draggable divider. It's genuinely relevant prior art: it stacks a full light copy under a full dark copy and animates `clip-path: inset(0 X% 0 0)` on the top layer via a `useMotionValue` + `useTransform` (deliberately bypassing React re-renders — motion writes straight to the DOM per frame). It uses `onPan` rather than `drag="x"` for reasons documented inline. It's a *demo* of the concept inside a case study, not the site's actual theme switcher, but it's the same conceptual model (two stacked theme copies, one clipped).

Other heavy-animation references, all motion/react and all in `src/components/watchtower-demo/`: `TerminalConversation.tsx`, `useTypewriter.ts`, `useStreamingReveal.ts`, `Bars.tsx`, `MeasureBar.tsx`.

## 7. prefers-reduced-motion

Yes, respected consistently, via three distinct patterns.

**Pattern A — shared JS helper** (`/Users/trounceabout/Documents/Repos/Personal/internal-transit/src/lib/animations.ts`, lines 3–5):
```ts
export function prefersReducedMotion() {
  return window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)").matches;
}
```
Used by `runFadeUpStagger` (same file, lines 11–16 — sets `opacity: 1` and returns early) and by the theme toggle in `Footer.astro:82`, where it **skips `startViewTransition` entirely** and just flips the class instantly. This is the pattern to preserve in your dissolve. `Lightbox.astro:110-111` re-declares the same function locally rather than importing it.

**Pattern B — CSS `no-preference` gate** (only animate when the user hasn't asked otherwise). `global.css:290-294`:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
  [data-animate] {
    opacity: 0;
  }
}
```
Same shape in `src/pages/index.astro:177-184` (gates the project-row hover transitions).

**Pattern C — CSS `reduce` collapse-duration.** `src/components/Lightbox.astro:88-93` and `src/components/case-study/LightboxImage.astro:105`:
```css
@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
  .lightbox-backdrop,
  .lightbox-content {
    transition-duration: 0.01ms;
  }
}
```

**Pattern D — React hook.** `useReducedMotion()` from `motion/react` in `TerminalConversation.tsx:242`, `useStreamingReveal.ts:41`, `useTypewriter.ts:52`, `DocsViewerSandbox.tsx:54`, `UndercurrentSandbox.tsx:23`.

Notably, the existing theme-sweep CSS in `global.css` (lines 303–341) has **no** reduced-motion guard of its own — it relies entirely on the JS early-return in `Footer.astro`. If you move to a CSS-driven or overlay-driven dissolve, keep that JS guard or add a CSS one.

