/* Two composited mask layers, combined with `add` (visible where EITHER is
   visible):

     1. SOLID (linear-gradient) — a hard stop. Everything the sweep has fully
        passed, with nothing knocked out of it. Sized to 200% of the viewport
        so sliding it a full 100% carries the edge from one side of the
        screen to the other.
     2. NOISE (SVG feTurbulence, inlined as a data URI) — genuinely random
        blocky cells, confined to a --dither-band-tall strip riding just
        ahead of the solid edge.

   `add` is what makes the band terminate: behind the edge layer 1 is opaque
   and wins outright, so the page is solidly swept. Only inside the band,
   where layer 1 has ended, does the noise decide things cell by cell.

   Why feTurbulence and not tiled CSS gradients: any arrangement of
   conic/linear gradients is a *tiling*, so it repeats — and the eye picks up
   the lattice immediately at this scale, reading as crosshatch texture
   rather than scattered pixels. Turbulence is Perlin noise with no repeat
   period. feComponentTransfer's discrete tableValues then quantizes the
   smooth noise into hard on/off cells, and baseFrequency sets how large one
   cell is (lower = chunkier).

   Only mask-position animates — animating mask-size would stretch the cells
   visibly mid-sweep. */
[data-theme-sweep-direction]::view-transition-new(root) {
  mask-size:
    100% 200%,
    100% var(--dither-band);
  mask-repeat: no-repeat, repeat-x;
  mask-composite: add;
  animation-duration: var(--theme-sweep-duration);
  animation-timing-function: linear;
  animation-fill-mode: forwards;
}

[data-theme-sweep-direction="light"]::view-transition-new(root) {
  mask-image:
    linear-gradient(to top, #000 0 50%, transparent 50%),
    var(--dither-noise);
  animation-name: theme-sweep-up;
}

[data-theme-sweep-direction="dark"]::view-transition-new(root) {
  mask-image:
    linear-gradient(to bottom, #000 0 50%, transparent 50%),
    var(--dither-noise);
  animation-name: theme-sweep-down;
}

/* Both layers travel together so the noise band stays locked to the leading
   edge. The band is offset by its own height so it rides just *ahead* of the
   solid region rather than overlapping it. */
@keyframes theme-sweep-up {
  from {
    mask-position:
      0 200%,
      0 calc(100% + var(--dither-band));
  }
  to {
    mask-position:
      0 100%,
      0 calc(0% - var(--dither-band));
  }
}

@keyframes theme-sweep-down {
  from {
    mask-position:
      0 -100%,
      0 calc(0% - var(--dither-band));
  }
  to {
    mask-position:
      0 0%,
      0 calc(100% + var(--dither-band));
  }
}

